+      <div class="journal-content" id="journal-text">
+        <p>
+          Every exceptional cup of The Daily Grind starts thousands of miles away on misty mountain slopes. Rather than buying commodity lots through opaque broker networks, we cultivate direct-trade relationships with multigenerational family farmers in the volcanic highlands of Huila, Colombia, the lush shade canopies of Yirgacheffe, Ethiopia, and the mineral-rich soils of Antigua, Guatemala. Each harvest season, our roasting team travels directly to these origin fincas to walk the hillsides, cup experimental micro-lots alongside producers, and understand the microclimates that shape each varietal's distinctive flavor profile.
+        </p>
+
+        <h3>Selective Hand-Harvesting at High Elevation</h3>
+        <p>
+          Great coffee is forged on the branch. Our partner farms sit at extreme altitudes between 1,700 and 2,100 meters above sea level, where cool mountain nights slow cherry maturation and concentrate organic sugars. Our partner farmers practice 100% selective hand-picking, harvesting only deep-crimson cherries at peak brix density. While mechanical strip-harvesting is common on industrial plantations, hand-selection ensures unripe or overripe fruit never dilutes the cup. This meticulous labor requires multiple passes through the same groves over several weeks, honoring the natural rhythm of the crop and ensuring superior sweetness, bright acidity, and clarity in the final roast.
+        </p>
+
+        <h3>Regenerative Agriculture & Closed-Loop Processing</h3>
+        <p>
+          True artisanal coffee cannot thrive at the expense of the earth. We collaborate exclusively with farms employing regenerative agroforestry practices—preserving native shade trees that shelter migratory birds, promote soil biodiversity, and protect fragile topsoils from erosion. At the wet mills, our partners have pioneered closed-loop eco-pulpers and anaerobic honey fermentation techniques that reduce water consumption by more than 80% compared to traditional washed methods. Waste coffee cherry pulp is subsequently composted back into nutrient-rich organic fertilizer for the following season's seedlings.
+        </p>
+
+        <h3>Fair Value Compensation & Rigorous Cupping Standards</h3>
+        <p>
+          Sustainability is as much economic as it is ecological. We commit to multi-year purchase agreements, paying premiums that average 40% above Fair Trade minimum pricing to ensure living wages and fund local community infrastructure, from clean water filtration to rural schooling. Once green coffee arrives at our roasting facility, we roast test samples in 250g micro-batches on our vintage drum roaster. Every single lot must achieve an 86+ Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) cupping score across fragrance, body, balance, and clean finish before it is cleared for our signature blends and single-origin releases.
+        </p>
